<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as RSS, National Volunteer Union</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh (RSS) is an Indian right-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> volunteer paramilitary organisation, founded on 27 September 1925 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> by K. B. Hedgewar, a doctor influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</a>'s Essentials of Hindutva. It set out to unite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> community and establish a Hindu Rashtra, and has been accused of intolerance of minorities, particularly Muslims. It played no role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement. It was banned in 1948 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathuram-godse">Nathuram Godse</a>, a member, assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, again during the Emergency and after the demolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babri-masjid">Babri Masjid</a> in 1992. It leads the Sangh Parivar, which includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatiya-janata-party">Bharatiya Janata Party</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rashtriya-swayamsevak-sangh.jpg" alt="Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Path_Sanchalan_Bhopal-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
